WEBVTT

00:00:00.184 --> 00:00:04.657
First Normal Form removes repeating groups and requires each field to represent one value.

00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:10.277
Instead of storing several service codes in one cell, each appointment-service occurrence gets its own row.

00:00:10.277 --> 00:00:15.537
That immediately makes each service easier to filter, validate, and connect to other tables.

00:00:15.537 --> 00:00:18.662
But 1NF does not solve every design problem.

00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:22.312
In the example, patient information, provider information, appointment information,

00:00:22.312 --> 00:00:23.642
and service information are still repeated across several rows.

00:00:23.642 --> 00:00:27.802
So think of 1NF as the beginning of the normalization process.

00:00:27.802 --> 00:00:33.532
We have removed lists inside fields, but we still need to ask which columns depend on which parts of the key.

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:37.642
Connect this idea to a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:00:37.642 --> 00:00:42.807
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:42.807 --> 00:00:47.652
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:47.652 --> 00:00:51.442
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:51.442 --> 00:00:57.040
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
